WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.400
"Are Onions Beneficial for Testosterone,
Osteoporosis, Allergies, and Cancer?"

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:13.360
Onions are potentially a
good source of antioxidants,

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.240
though interestingly, the
antioxidants are concentrated

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:20.000
in the outer layers immediately
under the papery peel.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.440
Unfortunately, most consumers
discard these most nutrient-rich

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.720
outermost layers, thus
losing a valuable part.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.720
Here are some numbers.
Look at that!

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.960
More than 10 times
more antioxidants in the

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.480
outer layer of white onions,
compared to the core.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.720
You’ll also note that
yellow onions in general

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.480
have more antioxidants than white.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:41.920
And red onions beat
them both, based on three

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.160
different antioxidant testing methods.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.720
That’s why I always try to buy red.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.280
Though red onions are
indeed slightly better, yellow

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.760
or white onions are no
slouches, containing

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.800
considerable levels of
antioxidant activity.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.160
So, nutritious, sure, but
are there any particular

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.720
clinical benefits to onion consumption?

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:04.000
There are grammatically
challenged titles like this

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.440
in the medical literature purporting

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.520
all sorts of miraculous benefits.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:10.240
But what do they base these claims on?

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.200
For example, here’s a review
purporting to have evidence

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.880
that testosterone in males
are enhanced by onion,

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.680
but the researchers were
talking about studies like this on

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:25.120
the effects of onion juice on
testicular torsion . . . in rats.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.760
Who cares what happens after
a rat testicle is rotated

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.880
720 degrees counterclockwise?
(Except, of course, the rat.)

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:37.200
You don’t know what happens
in people until you put

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.960
human testes to the test and . . .

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.800
onion extracts don’t appear to work.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.480
Okay, what about bone health?

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.200
Evidently, older white women
who consumed onions at least

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.880
once a day had an overall bone
density that was 5% greater than

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:57.323
individuals who consumed onions
no more than once a month (P &lt; 0.03).

00:01:57.323 --> 00:01:59.680
Now 5% might not sound like
a lot, but that improvement in

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.480
bone density could potentially
translate into decreasing

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.240
their risk of hip fracture
by more than 20%

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.400
if indeed, it was cause and effect.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.480
Daily administration of onion did
cause a big bump in bone density.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.840
This opens the possibility for
a low-cost, safe, and effective

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.480
nutritional approach
to osteoporosis and—

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.732
you guessed it...in the rat.
Another rodent study.

00:02:22.732 --> 00:02:24.400
Rats!

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.231
But finally, here we go.

00:02:26.231 --> 00:02:27.840
Tremendous strides have been made

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.280
in treating osteoporosis with
drugs, but they have the potential

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.080
for serious adverse side effects,
so scientists have drawn

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.920
their attention to natural remedies.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.280
So, let’s randomize people
to drink... onion juice

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.920
or placebo onion juice.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.280
I don’t know what sounds worse:
sugary onion juice

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.160
or fake sugary onion juice.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.200
And as if drinking onion
juice wasn’t bad enough,

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.344
it was all for nothing.
It didn’t even work.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.840
What about the antiallergy
activities of shallots

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.160
and any therapeutic effects
on helping allergic runny noses?

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.880
Sixteen such patients were
randomized equally into

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.080
an antihistamine group or a
group that got antihistamines

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.680
plus some capsules containing
dried shallot powder.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.280
And it looked like the
shallot group did better,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:23.040
but there was actually no
statistically significant difference

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.280
in total symptoms
between the two groups;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.960
so, another #OnionFail.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.760
There has to be something
onions can do.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.120
What about testing the effects of
fresh yellow onion consumption

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.960
on breast cancer patients to try
to decrease the toxic effects

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.000
of a chemotherapy drug
called doxorubicin?

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Unfortunately, no significant
benefit was found on decreasing

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.680
damage to the liver or heart.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.920
But here we go . . . finally,
a clinical benefit to onions.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.720
The consumption of fresh
yellow onion ameliorates

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.520
the high blood sugars and
insulin resistance in breast cancer

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.200
patients during doxorubicin-
based chemotherapy.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.080
Doxorubicin isn’t just toxic
to the liver and heart,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.040
but it may also contribute
to insulin resistance.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.760
So, let’s do a randomized, triple-blind,
controlled clinical trial

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.480
randomizing patients to
like a whole onion a day

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.520
or a third of an onion
a day. What happened?

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.720
The high onion group experienced
a significant decrease

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.640
in blood sugars and insulin resistance

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.800
compared to the low onion group.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:29.520
They went up in the low onion group
but down in the high onion group.

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:32.320
So, make onions your friend.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.702
What’s the worst that can happen—
a little onion breath and BO?

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.840
Probably the least of your worries
if you have cancer on chemotherapy.

